"""
# Service Externs — UDS (Unix Domain Socket) SFFI declarations

Declares the runtime externs for UDS server/client operations and the
text-shaped wrappers callers should use.

## ABI contract

The runtime implements these symbols with a **(ptr, len)** ABI, plus an
out-parameter for `recv` and a companion `rt_unix_socket_free_buf`:

    rt_unix_socket_listen(path_ptr, path_len, backlog) -> fd | -errno
    rt_unix_socket_connect(path_ptr, path_len)         -> fd | -errno
    rt_unix_socket_accept(fd)                          -> cfd | -EAGAIN | -errno
    rt_unix_socket_send(fd, data_ptr, data_len)        -> nbytes | -errno
    rt_unix_socket_recv(fd, max_len, out_len_ptr)      -> buf_ptr (0 on EOF/err)
    rt_unix_socket_free_buf(buf_ptr, len)
    rt_unix_socket_close(fd)                           -> 0 | -errno

Both lanes agree on this shape: the native runtime in
`src/compiler_rust/runtime/src/value/net_uds.rs` and the interpreter shims in
`src/compiler_rust/compiler/src/interpreter_extern/qmp_socket.rs`.

Declaring these with text-shaped signatures (`path: text`) is a **defect**: a
tagged interpreter string is passed where a raw byte pointer is expected, so
`listen`/`connect` return -22 (EINVAL) and `recv` dereferences a bogus
out-parameter and core-dumps. See
`doc/08_tracking/bug/rt_unix_socket_extern_shape_mismatch_2026-08-26.md`.

Simple text is bridged to a raw pointer with `rt_string_data`, and a received
buffer is lifted back to text with `rt_string_new`.
"""

use std.io_runtime

# --- UDS externs ---
# Client-side (already in runtime via qmp_client)
extern fn rt_unix_socket_connect(path: text) -> i64

# Server-side (new — require runtime implementation)
extern fn rt_unix_socket_listen(path: text, backlog: i64) -> i64
extern fn rt_unix_socket_accept(fd: i64) -> i64
extern fn rt_unix_socket_send(fd: i64, data: text) -> i64
extern fn rt_unix_socket_recv(fd: i64, max_bytes: i64) -> text
extern fn rt_unix_socket_close(fd: i64) -> bool
